      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface detection by anti-FLAG antibody staining without permeabilization confirmed plasma-membrane trafficking in all three studies. Water-channel function was not explicitly re-assayed in the FLAG-tagged constructs in the cited lines, but surface delivery was intact.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "Python computation confirms Q at position 43 and T at position 44 in the canonical 269-aa AQP1 sequence. ECL1 spans residues 34\u201349 (between TM1 end at 33 and TM2 start at 50). Q43 is 10 residues from the TM1 boundary and T44 is 6 residues from the TM2 boundary, both well above the \u22653-residue minimum. All three ledger lines explicitly name the Q43\u2013T44 junction in full-length, membrane-anchored constructs and demonstrate extracellular tag exposure by non-permeabilized antibody staining. The junction is validated at single-residue resolution by multiple independent papers. No ambiguity in numbering frame: the canonical isoform has no signal peptide, so UniProt positions map directly. Three independent publications (PMC12802846, PMC11253201, PMC10274688) all report insertion of a FLAG epitope tag into the first extracellular loop (ECL1) of full-length, membrane-anchored human or Leishmania-species AQP1 immediately after Q43 (between Q43 and T44). In each case, the tagged protein was detected at the cell surface using anti-FLAG antibodies without cell permeabilization, confirming plasma-membrane trafficking and extracellular exposure of the tag. PMC11253201 used an AQP1-FKBP12-DD fusion; PMC10274688 used LSAqp1 (a close ortholog). All constructs are full-length and membrane-anchored. [validation: surface_only; position: validated; source: paper; entailment_verified: True]",
      "sources": [
